    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The correlation, RMSE and 1° map comparison require all four nitrate fields to share one mask and to be scored only where every source has data, as this fragment specifies.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build one mask over all four nitracline maps on the common 1° grid with `build_comparison_mask` and apply `.where(mask)` to every field before any correlation/RMSE; the default `ocean_only=True` is correct here because WOA23 is the reference (for a GLODAP reference it would drop good cells).",
        "Report the cell counts the mask records alongside the metrics; a mask much smaller than the individual fields means the products disagree about where the ocean is, not that the models are poor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "basin-and-region-definitions",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/basin-and-region-definitions.md",
      "sha256": "d0d90c32fab9f6e0a39d4f769a4e902e6412b2ad1b09d9c7d19f6bb3aa426611",
      "reason": "The basin means depend on the WOA23 basin partition, marginal-sea folding and exclusion of Southern/Arctic basins, and the fragment requires stating which partition was used.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "All four sources must use one basin mask: WOA23 `basinmask_01` via `load_basin_mask`/`select_basin`, with `include_marginal_seas=True` (the default) folding marginal seas into their parent ocean; state that this is a WOA labelling, not the OMIP 10-region one, and that the Southern and Arctic are separate basins.",
        "Use the surface labels through the water column (`depth_dependent=False`), which is what a named ocean usually means; per-level labels are for water-mass analyses. State the choice, since it changes basin means."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "The nitracline is a nonlinear profile diagnostic, so it should be computed on each model's native grid and regridded only for maps and pattern comparisons, since regridding changes masks and conservation.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Take the global and basin mean nitracline depths on each source's own grid with its own `areacello`; regrid to the WOA 1° grid only for the maps, the pattern correlation and the RMSE on the shared mask, with `regrid_horizontal` 'conservative' going coarser, and state the method.",
        "The nitracline is a vertical-extremum diagnostic quantified (and thus quantised) by each source's own vertical grid — where the profile is smooth the maximum gradient can jump between levels — so read the maps together with the gradient and state this caveat."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-bgc-units-per-volume",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-bgc-units-per-volume.md",
      "sha256": "30d729792e1986dd91c822785512ceabe336ef10a47f0a7db602802e5425d70b",
      "reason": "CMIP6 no3 is stored per unit volume while WOA23 nitrate is per unit mass, so the shared concentration scale in Figure 2 and any nitrate comparison need the conversion this fragment describes.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`no3` is archived in mol m-3 and WOA23 reports µmol kg-1: Figure 2's single concentration unit requires a conversion using the in-situ density (`compute_potential_density_anomaly` with `ref_pressure=None`), and the answer must state the density used (`attrs['density_assumption']`); the constant default errs by about a per cent in the deep ocean.",
        "The nitracline depth itself is scale-invariant (a depth of an extremum/gradient), so no density conversion is needed for it; take it from the native field, since in-situ density rescales each level differently and shifts such a depth a little."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "Global and basin mean nitracline depths must be area-weighted over ocean cells rather than simple grid-cell averages, following the weighting rule in this fragment.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Global and basin mean nitracline depths must be area-weighted: Σ depth·`areacello` / Σ `areacello` over valid cells only, with NaN-aware weights, via `compute_basic_stat(op='area_weighted_mean')`; an unweighted `mean()` over (y,x) or over latitude is wrong.",
        "Map statistics are weighted too: `compute_difference_metrics` weights by area by default, and `analyze_correlation` needs `weighting='area'` for the pattern correlation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "monthly-means-are-time-averages",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/monthly-means-are-time-averages.md",
      "sha256": "df6b5c23460f88f1522e5c6a665e1efbb8322c3ae876c885530f3019aa284ee0",
      "reason": "The nitracline is a nonlinear diagnostic of the monthly-mean nitrate fields, and this fragment warns that computing it offline from monthly means differs from averaging the diagnostic.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "f(monthly mean) ≠ mean of f: a nitracline from monthly means is not the time mean of the monthly nitraclines. State which order was used; \"average first, then diagnostic\" is the like-for-like choice because the reference exists only as a WOA climatology.",
        "Averaging the monthly fields over 1971-2000 requires day weights in each model's calendar: `temporal_aggregate`, not `compute_basic_stat(op='mean', dim='time')`, which weights every month equally."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "missing-values-and-land-cells",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "pitfalls/missing-values-and-land-cells.md",
      "sha256": "f576244f13dde06fed1777eaa9721c68844f61758f66fac704b44f1551e90df2",
      "reason": "Every CMIP6 read must CF-mask the 1e20 land and below-sea-floor values, otherwise those huge numbers dominate the gradient search and the means.",
      "status": "dropped",
      "notes": [
        "Build each field's valid mask from that field itself (masks differ between variables of the same model) and keep weights masked to the same cells; any value near 1e20 in output is an unmasked fill value.",
        "A hand-rolled weighted reduction returns 0.0 at all-land columns (xarray's sum skips NaN); use `temporal_aggregate`, which masks each cell's weights to the steps it has data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "GFDL-ESM4 ocean output is gr (conservatively regridded to 1°×1°) while CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are gn, which affects masks, conservation and which regridding operations are exact.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4 output is `gr`, already remapped to 1° (conserve_order1) with a regridded land–sea mask, so its \"native\" numbers are 1°-archive numbers; CESM2 is `gn` ~100 km and MPI-ESM1-2-LR ~250 km, so features narrower than the coarse grid are not comparable in amplitude."
      ]
    }
